@prefix sim:  <http://example.org/orexis/sim#> .
@prefix : <http://example.org/orexis/sim#> .
@prefix orexis:   <http://example.org/orexis#> .
@prefix ssn:  <http://www.w3.org/ns/ssn/> .
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix owl:  <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix xsd:  <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<http://example.org/orexis/sim> a owl:Ontology ;
    rdfs:label "Stand-in" ;
    rdfs:comment """What stands in for something that is not there, and the physics it computes.

A knowledge-only package: no Python, no capability, nothing to grant.

**A stand-in is not a device that happens to be fake.** It is the absence of one. `sim:simulatedBy`
takes `ssn:System` — the thing PLAYING the role, a sensor or an actuator or a board — and says the
role has no referent: nobody built it, a process supplies its behaviour. Attaching that to a
physical class was the error this package exists to correct, because it made every world assert a
physical edge node and then deny it in the next line (a-stand-in-is-not-a-device).

The generators read all of it and nothing an agent runs does. An agent must not be able to tell,
and the surest way is that nothing it executes ever asks.""" .

#################  The claim itself  #################

:simulatedBy a owl:ObjectProperty ; rdfs:domain ssn:System ; rdfs:range :Model ;
    rdfs:label "simulated by" ;
    rdfs:comment "No such thing exists; a process stands in for it, publishing on the same topics and speaking the same protocol. The mark is on the SYSTEM and never on the agent, and that is the whole design: an agent that could tell would be a second code path, and a second code path is what a simulation is supposed to avoid — world/simulation used to exercise its own implementation, so it could pass while the real one was broken. The domain is ssn:System rather than a physical class because what is stood in for is a ROLE — this observes, this acts — and a role's referent is exactly what a stand-in denies." .

:Model a owl:Class ; rdfs:label "Model" ;
    rdfs:comment "The physics the stand-in computes between readings. Facts about the thing being modelled rather than opinions about it, which is why they sit on the system and not in anybody's beliefs." .

#################  The physics  #################

:minValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "minimum" ;
    rdfs:comment "The floor of what this stand-in can report. A moisture fraction happens to run 0..1; a thermometer does not, so the range is stated rather than assumed." .
:maxValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ; rdfs:label "maximum" .

:initialValue a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Model ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "initial value" ;
    rdfs:comment "What it reads before anything has happened to it." .

:doseEffect a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "dose effect" ;
    rdfs:comment "How a dispensed unit moves this modelled value, SIGNED — in units of this value per litre on the dose wire, the litre being the WIRE's unit (a valve's status message speaks ml; that is actuation's physical reality, so it lives in this comment and not in the term's name). A pot's conversion arrives through the domain's denomination and never through this; a SOURCE'S effect is not per-instance configuration at all but CONSERVATION — every source loses exactly what was dispensed from it — so the water domain states -1.0 once, on the WaterSource class, and the closure entails it onto every barrel. Read from the SUBJECT by the generation, like the ceiling and the drying." .

:losesPerDay a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    # Un-domained since #164: stated on a Model as an override, or entailed onto a SUBJECT from a
    # domain's own physics term (water:driesPerDay rdfs:subPropertyOf this), so the generator
    # reads one term and the pot keeps the only copy of its fact.
    rdfs:label "loses per day" ;
    rdfs:comment "How much of the observed property is LOST per simulated day — first-order decay toward the model's floor, for any value that drains: a pot's moisture, a barrel's evaporation, a battery's self-discharge. Was a per-TICK rate once, and the tick made the physics an artifact of attention: the pot dried one step per reading, so a closely-watched plant dried faster than an ignored one — backwards, and visibly so once the cadence machinery started varying how closely anyone watched. Time-based now, scaled by the world's sim:timeScale, so the pot loses what the day costs it however often it is read. Since #164 the domain's water:driesPerDay is a subproperty of this: a pot states its physics once, in its own vocabulary, and the closure entails the term the generation reads — while a Model may still state this directly, as an override for values that are not anyone's pot." .

:dailySwing a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Model ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "daily swing" ;
    rdfs:comment "The amplitude of a daily sine around the value — for properties with a diurnal cycle, which soil moisture does not have and air temperature does. One simulated day per period, so under sim:timeScale 24 a bench hour draws a whole day's curve. Absent means flat, which is what the old ever-falling drift pretended not to be." .

:tickSeconds a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain :Model ; rdfs:range xsd:integer ;
    rdfs:label "tick seconds" ;
    rdfs:comment "Wall-clock seconds per tick — the PUBLISH interval of a sensing:PushProcedure stand-in, which keeps its own clock; a sensing:ScheduledProcedure one keeps the interval its agent gives it, exactly as a board does. This used to also pace the physics, which is the artifact sim:losesPerDay records; physics run on the clock now, and the tick is only a rhythm of speech." .

#################  Facts about a simulated WORLD  #################

:timeScale a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:World ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "time scale" ;
    rdfs:comment "Simulated seconds per wall-clock second — 24 makes one bench hour a simulated day. A fact about the WORLD, not any one stand-in: every stand-in in a world must age at the same rate or their physics stop composing (a dose sized for a day's drying must land against a day's drying). The agents are deliberately not told: they act in real time against a world that happens to live fast, which changes nothing about how they decide and everything about how much of their behaviour a bench hour shows." .

:strayDoseMeanDays a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:domain orexis:World ; rdfs:range xsd:decimal ;
    rdfs:label "stray dose mean days" ;
    rdfs:comment "The mean interval, in simulated days, between waterings NOBODY in the society decided — a human with a watering can, rain through a window. Exponentially distributed, per subject that states a sim:rainTopic. Absent means nobody meddles. Stating it gives the world ONE meddler: a container of its own (firmware/simulated-meddler) with a credential of its own, granted WRITE on each rain topic and nothing else — a pot must not water itself, and rain must never ride the valve's status topic, which is the valve's testimony. This is the third-party impulse of #151 made routine: the signal changes without any agent's doing, and the society's only defence is how it samples." .

:rainTopic a owl:DatatypeProperty ; rdfs:range xsd:string ;
    rdfs:label "rain topic" ;
    rdfs:comment "Where water from OUTSIDE the society arrives for this subject — the meddler writes it, the subject's stand-in reads it, and nothing else may touch it. On the SUBJECT, because rain falls on a pot and not on an instrument; a simulated world without one for a subject keeps that subject dry except by the market. The payload is the dose report's shape ({\"ml\": N}) because millilitres are millilitres to soil — the indistinguishability is the point, not an economy." .
